  "result": "`summarize()` in `src/report.js` now handles 200,000 orders well inside the 1-second budget, and its output is unchanged. `npm test` passes, with all 4 tests green.\n\nThere were two slow spots:\n- **Per-order lookups:** the duplicate-order check and the customer lookup both scanned a growing array for every order. They now use a `Set` and a `Map`.\n- **Sorting inside the loop:** the leaderboard was re-sorted after every order. It is now sorted once at the end. This gives the same ordering because `byRank` ties are broken by `customerId`, and every customer appears only once.\n\nSome behavior I kept deliberately:\n- An order ID is marked as seen even if the order is refunded, so a later duplicate of a refunded order is still ignored.\n- The output fields keep the same names and order.\n\nI added two tests:\n- **Behavior test:** it covers duplicate IDs, a refunded ID that is later re-sent as paid, EUR conversion, pending orders, and ties on spend, first-order time and customer ID.\n- **Timing test:** it runs 200,000 orders and fails if `summarize()` takes 1 second or more.\n\nI also ran the new tests against the old code. The timing test fails there (the runner's 5-second limit hits first). I didn't separately check the behavior test against the old code, but it passes on the new code. To exercise the logic changes, I'd also check the new `summarize()` against the old one on random inputs, but I didn't do that here.",
